{
 "id": "2026-11-25-a",
 "title": "Skyscrapers stand still? This one hides a 660-ton ball 🏙️",
 "description": "Tall buildings sway in the wind, and Taipei 101 (508 m) fights back with the world's largest tuned mass damper: a 660-tonne steel ball made of 41 steel plates, hanging between the 87th and 92nd floors. When the tower moves one way, the heavy ball lags and swings the other way, and hydraulic pistons turn that motion into heat. The builder says it cuts the sway by up to 40%. Its biggest recorded swing was about 1 metre, during Typhoon Soudelor in August 2015 (design maximum: 1.5 m).\n\nSources: Guinness World Records (largest tuned mass damper); Business Insider via Yahoo News; Taiwan News; Boing Boing.",
